explanation:
stationaryPart x = x − w, where w ∈ S solves ⟪A w, v⟫ = ⟪A x, v⟫ for all v ∈ S. w is obtained through the coercive inverse of the compressed operator P_S A|_S applied to P_S(Ax), which is Lax–Milgram on S. Lemmas in the file confirm the expected properties:
- the result satisfies the stationary equations;
- it differs from x by an element of S;
- it vanishes on S;
- for symmetric A, it minimises the energy in its class.

endpointExtension composes this projection with the trial lift L, which matches its docstring and its use in building the Dirichlet-to-Neumann operator Eᵀ A E. Degenerate cases behave correctly: S = 0 gives L, and S = E gives 0.

The setting (real Hilbert spaces, complete subspace S, bounded coercive A) is the natural Lax–Milgram setting. Mathlib has no oblique-projection or Dirichlet-to-Neumann extension operator of this kind, only building blocks such as IsCoercive.continuousLinearEquivOfBilin and orthogonal projections. So the definition is not too narrow.
